# A 二重ヲ格 sentence whose second を-phrase spans two nominal tokens, which
# is what makes one span admit two capture assignments. From start 0
# double_wo_dep_skeleton's `[!@DW_DEP_WO]{0,8}` gap may consume 理想 or
# nothing, so $n2 binds 社会的 or 理想 and both reach 掲げる. Only 社会的
# satisfies the clause — を's dep.head is 3 — and it is the one the
# evaluator reaches *second*, because `repeat` yields levels longest-first
# and the longer gap is the failing binding.
#
# That order is why this shape and not a head-final compound. A correct
# Japanese compound attaches を to its last token, which puts the satisfying
# assignment first and leaves the defect unobservable; measured, and the
# discarded 実験機材 witness did exactly that. The annotation here is the
# analyzer's, not an annotator's: GiNZA parses a 的-nominal head-initial,
# and this is sentence 329 of the re-validation's parse reduced, the same
# reduction docs/design/2026-08-29-where-capture-assignment.md reproduces.
# Committing the parse the corpus produced is the point — the 32 sentences
# the collapse cost were real parses, not idealized ones.
#
# The four sentences above cannot express this: one を-phrase has one
# assignment. Without it the counted-work screen saw no candidate collapse
# on either relation-bearing catalog. Narrowing the deduplication key back
# to the position alone now drops this catalog's record and moves its
# candidate counters.
#
# Degraded for native speakers, deliberately: the 二重ヲ格制約 is what the
# catalog exists to find, so a witness that satisfied it would be measuring
# something else. Both を-phrases govern 掲げる, the head-sharing fact the
# clause tests.
# sent_id = witness-double-wo
# text = 政策を社会的理想を掲げる。
1	政策	政策	NOUN	_	_	6	obj	_	_
2	を	を	ADP	_	_	1	case	_	_
3	社会的	社会的	NOUN	_	_	6	obj	_	_
4	理想	理想	NOUN	_	_	3	compound	_	_
5	を	を	ADP	_	_	3	case	_	_
6	掲げる	掲げる	VERB	_	_	0	root	_	_
7	。	。	PUNCT	_	_	6	punct	_	_
